{{- /*
The account-wide half of a Codex enrolment, merged into ~/.codex/hooks.json,
which Codex reads for every directory it works in.

A hook and nothing beside it. Codex's own `.rules` files are an exec policy:
they decide commands and name no path, so there is no file an install can put a
deny rule for ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 in. The hook is what refuses a file tool, and
`faramir guard --host codex` refuses one by the shape of the call rather than by
the tool's name.

Deny-only, and that is the whole of the difference from the tree's. A rewrite
has to be approved to run, and the allow that approves it approves every command
the list does not name. So routing costs an approval, and an operator takes that
trade per tree. What this one does is refuse what the list names, wherever the
agent is working, and say nothing about anything else: a silent answer leaves
Codex's own approval flow as it was.

The matcher is "*" rather than "Bash". An empty reply is a call left alone, so
answering for a tool that runs nothing costs nothing, and matching every tool is
what puts apply_patch and the file tools in front of the deny list at all.

Codex does not run a hook it has not been told to trust, and says nothing when
it skips one. See the note `faramir init` prints.
*/ -}}
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "*",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": {{ quote (printf "%s/faramir guard --host codex --deny-only" .BinDir) }},
            "timeout": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
